# Project: Adam Donkin's Coaching Platform

## Tech Stack (verified)
- Next.js 15 with App Router (`frontend/src/app/`)
- TypeScript
- Supabase (Postgres + Auth + Edge Functions)
- Tailwind CSS v4 with `@tailwindcss/postcss`
- shadcn/ui (`frontend/src/components/ui/`)
- Radix UI primitives (@radix-ui/colors, dialog, dropdown-menu, select, separator, avatar, slot)
- Lucide React for icons
- date-fns for date formatting
- Geist font family
- Deployed on Vercel

## Coding Conventions
- **Match existing patterns first.** Before implementing anything, check if the codebase already solves a similar problem and use that approach. Don't take shortcuts (e.g., hardcoding values) when a correct, dynamic pattern already exists. Only deviate with a concrete reason (performance, complexity), not convenience.
- **Enum values come from the database; display metadata lives in code.** Dropdowns and option lists pull values from Supabase via `get_enum_values` RPC. Labels, colors, and icons for those values are mapped in code.
- Read existing files before writing new ones.
- Server components for data fetching (see `frontend/src/app/page.tsx` — uses `createServerComponentClient`).
- API routes delegate to Supabase Edge Functions (see `frontend/src/app/api/sync-calendar/route.ts`).
- Supabase client is in `frontend/src/lib/supabaseClient.ts` — uses anon key, client-side.
- Complex queries use RPC functions (`supabase.rpc('function_name')`).
- Client type interface lives in `frontend/src/components/types.ts`.
- Migrations are timestamped SQL files: `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_description.sql` in `supabase/migrations/`.
- RLS is enabled on all tables. Data tables use `accessible_user_ids()` for SELECT and `can_write_user_data()` for writes (supports team access via `team_access` table). New tables must follow the same pattern.
- Keep API routes thin — business logic in separate modules.
- Error handling: always return meaningful error messages, log errors server-side.
- Environment variables: use NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix only for client-side values.

## Existing Tables
- `clients` — id (uuid), user_id, name, email, company_name, role, monthly_fee, is_active, status (enum: active/pending/waiting/inactive/staff)
- `sessions` — id, date, notes, client_id (FK), calendar_event_id, status
- `calendar_events` — Google Calendar integration
- `user_tokens` — OAuth tokens for Google Calendar
- `client_sync_status` — Tracks sync state
- `session_notes` — Session workspace content (connection_notes, topics_content)
- `client_actions` — Action items with rich-text descriptions (description_content jsonb)
- `team_access` — Multi-user access (owner_id, member_id, role: viewer/editor)

## Design Principles
- Clean, minimal UI. No decorative elements.
- Use shadcn/ui components as the base.
- Consistent spacing and typography with existing pages.
- Use 8px vertical rhythm for spacing (8, 16, 24, 32, 48px etc).
- Mobile-responsive — Adam checks this from his phone.
- Dark mode support (ThemeProvider exists in `frontend/src/components/theme/`).

## Project Context
This is a coaching platform for an executive coach. The primary user is Adam (the coach), not his clients.

We are extending it with:
- Zoom transcript archive (current build)
- AI-powered session analysis (future)
- Action/goal tracking (future)
- Client-facing portal (future)

## Important
- Never delete or overwrite existing functionality.
- Ask before refactoring existing code.
- All dates should be in Pacific Time (America/Los_Angeles).
- Supabase service role key is for server-side only — never expose to client.
- Auth uses Supabase Auth. All new tables need user_id column + RLS policies matching existing tables.
